rces for all i -> race conditions. Not good.\n\nMaybe use blocking over j to improve cache reuse of p[i] across multiple i? But that's what tile did and was slower.\n\nLet's examine the interactions: The best experiment (89b40ce89590426a, 6be2b0550d04446e, 497dddd10ed44d29) had score 0.812058 mean 0.155377. We don't know what those source IDs are, but they might be from other roles: maybe one is SoA, another OpenMP static, another something else. Our own role history shows we tried many and best was ~0.176 with SoA+OpenMP static (db1ea5cc8983401a). The interaction experiment that got 0.155 likely combined different ideas from other roles: maybe loop unrolling or better scheduling.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate. Let's think of a novel transformation not yet tried in our role history: Use OpenMP tasking? Probably overhead. Or use cache blocking with tile size tuned, but previous BI=8 was bad; try BI=4? But we don't know n (number of bodies) from input; could be large. The baseline is 0.67s for some test cases. We need to handle all sizes.\n\nMaybe combine SoA with OpenMP static and also use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static, chunk) where chunk size maybe 1? Static default divides evenly. Could try dynamic but that may change order? No, each i independent so any scheduling preserves per-i summation order as long as j loop is sequential within thread. So we can use dynamic or guided to improve load balance if n not divisible by threads. But static with default chunk size (n/threads) might have imbalance if n%4 !=0; but that's minor.\n\nMaybe use OpenMP parallel for with schedule(static, 1) to distribute round-robin? That would give each thread interleaved i values, which may improve cache locality because adjacent threads access nearby p[j] simultaneously? Actually all threads read the same p[j] array sequentially, so they share cache lines. Static contiguous chunks might cause each thread to stream through entire p array independently, causing more cache m