f(float));\n  float *vy = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *vz = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n    px[i] = bodies[i].x;\n    py[i] = bodies[i].y;\n    pz[i] = bodies[i].z;\n    vx[i] = bodies[i].vx;\n    vy[i] = bodies[i].vy;\n    vz[i] = bodies[i].vz;\n  }\n\n  for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n    body_force(px, py, pz, vx, vy, vz, DT, nbodies);\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n      px[i] += vx[i] * DT;\n      py[i] += vy[i] * DT;\n      pz[i] += vz[i] * DT;\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n    bodies[i].x = px[i];\n    bodies[i].y = py[i];\n    bodies[i].z = pz[i];\n    bodies[i].vx = vx[i];\n    bodies[i].vy = vy[i];\n    bodies[i].vz = vz[i];\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n\n  free(px); free(py); free(pz);\n  free(vx); free(vy); free(vz);\n  free(bodies);\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n\nWe need to include omp.h? Not necessary if using pragmas; but we should compile with -fopenmp. The Makefile likely already does that? We don't know, but the baseline probably uses OpenMP? Actually baseline is sequential maybe. But our previous experiments used OpenMP and compiled fine. So okay.\n\nWe must ensure no race conditions: body_force writes vx[i] etc for each i; each thread handles distinct i due to schedule(static,1) so safe. Position update also parallel with static default (contiguous chunks) but each i independent.\n\nPotential issue: The inner loop reads px[j], py[j], pz[j] sequentially; all threads read same arrays concurrently. With schedule(static,1), thread 0 handles i=0,4,8,...; thread 1 i=1,5,... etc. At any given time, they may be at different j positions because each i loop starts from j=0 independently. So they won't necessarily progress together. Actually each thread processes its own i sequentially through all j; so if one thread is ahead, it reads far ahead in px array while others behind. That could cause more cache misses than contiguous chunks where each thread streams a different portion? Wait with contiguous chunks (static default), thread 0 handles first n/4 i's, and for each i it loops over all j from 0 to n-1. So all threads are reading the same px array but at different times; they may be roughly synchronized if work per i is equal, so they read similar ranges concurrently. With static(1), thread 0 processes i=0 then i=4 etc; after finishing i=0 (all j), it moves to i=4 while others still on i=1..3? Actually all threads start at same time and each does one i at a time; since work per i is identical, they finish roughly together. So thread 0 finishes i=0 at same time as thread 1 finishes i=1 etc. Then they move to next set: thread 0 i=4, thread 1 i=5... They are still synchronized in j progression because each i loop starts fresh; but after finishing first batch, all threads have read entire px array once. So during the second batch, they again start from j=0 and stream through. This means at any moment, all threads are reading similar j ranges (since they progress together), which is good for shared cache. With contiguous chunks, thread 0 processes i=0..n/4-1 sequentially; it reads px array n/4 times fully, while other threads also read full array but interleaved in time? Actually each thread does its own i's one after another; they all start at same time and progress through their chunk. Since work per i equal, they finish roughly together, so during the whole run they are reading similar j ranges as well (all start at j=0 for first i). So both schedules likely have similar cache behavior. But static(1) may reduce load imbalance if n not divisible by 4? With contiguous chunks, thread 3 might get fewer elements; with static(1), distribution is round-robin so each gets ceil(n/4) or floor, difference at most 1. So better balance.\n\nWe'll go with schedule(static,1). Also we can try adding #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static,1) on position update too? 